    "product_id": "9db62a91-4791-4ec1-a145-cc7ee19b8176",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 010911\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n211 AM MST Tue Sep 1 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Dry and tranquil weather under mostly clear skies can be\n  expected mid-week before potentially another period of wet\n  weather materializes by the end of the week into the weekend.\n\n- Near to slightly below normal temperatures are expected\n  throughout much of the week. \n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nLatest GOES IR imagery shows a batch of high clouds associated with \nshowers and storms from yesterday is now progressing into NM as much \ndrier air is advecting into AZ under W-SW flow aloft. Although upper-\nlvl moisture has been scoured out, there is still plenty of residual \nmoisture in the lower levels with dew points still hovering in the \nlow to mid 60s across much of the region with the exception of SE \nCalifornia where dew points are in the 50s. We will continue to hold \nonto this moisture through the afternoon which could lead to an \nisolated storm or two developing over the typical terrain features \nincluding the Harquahala Mountains in La Paz County and the Table \nTop wilderness in western Maricopa County. High-res models are \nindicating this scenario unfolding for a few hours this afternoon, \nbut activity should wane early this evening with the loss of daytime \nheating. Further drying of the boundary layer can be anticipated on \nWednesday with mixing ratios falling to around 8 g/kg. This will \nlead to another quiet day across our forecast area with any isolated \nconvection relegated to the higher terrain of northern and \nsoutheastern AZ. \n\nA large trough of low pressure will remain situated over the West \nCoast through the middle of this week with the subtropical ridge \ndisplaced well east of the region. This pattern will result in 500 \nmb hghts remaining in a seasonable 587-590 dam range. In response, \ntemperatures are not expected to fluctuate much at all with lower\ndesert highs topping out in the upper 90s to lower 100s and lows \nin the upper 70s to mid 80s. Due to a slight enhancement of the \nmid-lvl jet, winds will become gustier each afternoon, especially\nacross SE California and the Lower Colorado River valley. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /THURSDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/... \nThe forecast becomes a bit more muddy heading into the end of this \nweek and through the weekend. Currently the NHC shows a TC likely \nforming over the E Pacific, south of the tip of the Baja Del Norte \nwithin the next 24-48 hrs. Most forecast models show the storm \nitself curving out into open waters, however there is good evidence \nthat at least some tropical moisture will get pulled up into Mexico \nand making it as far north as southern AZ as early as Thursday.\nRoughly 90% of the GEFS and EPS members show PWATs increasing from \n1.5\" on Thursday to around 1.8-2.0\" over portions of southcentral \nand southwest AZ by Friday or Saturday. Given this likely increase \nin moisture, NBM PoPs have increased to around 40-50% with the \ninitial moisture push late Thursday into Friday. Thereafter, PoPs \nremain quite low in a 20-30% range through the weekend which is \nmost likely due to uncertainty in timing and coverage of rainfall \nwithin the deterministic model output. \n\nAlthough there will be adequate moisture return, the positioning \nof the ridge/trough features over the west will play a key role in\nhow much rainfall may actually materialize. If the ridge \nstrengthens too much and retrogrades wwd, then the focus for \nheavier rainfall may be much further west into SoCal and north \ninto UT and CO as indicated by the latest ECMWF and GFS runs. It \nwill likely be at least 2 or 3 more days until we have a good idea\non what will happen late this week, and any forecast that does \nnot show a range of possible outcomes should be disregarded at \nthis time. \n\nUncertainty in the temperature forecast by this weekend is also \napparent as indicated by large (8-10 degree) spreads within the \nNBM interquartile range. Once our region does begin to draw in the\nPacific moisture, temperatures will likely dip down to around 3-5\ndegrees below normal by the end of this week. However, as ridging\naloft attempts to build over the area this weekend, temperatures \nmay warm closer to normal by Sunday or Monday. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 0515Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \n\nNo major aviation concerns are expected during the TAF period.\nWinds will return to following light and diurnal trends with\nvariability during directional shifts. There are hints of an\nisolated afternoon VCTS and later evening outflow, but current\nchances for either of these events is around 10% or less and\ntherefore have been excluded from the TAFs. Other than a FEW low-\nlevel bases around 7-10 kft, skies will be mostly clear.\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \n\nNo major aviation concerns are expected during the TAF period.\nWinds will follow familiar diurnal trends with perhaps some\nvariability occurring during directional switches. Other than a\nFEW bases around 10 kft, skies will be mostly clear.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nAfter abundant wetting rainfall occurred across south-central AZ\nyesterday, drier conditions will settle into the region today \nwith rain chances falling to around 10% or less through the middle\nof this week. Better area-wide rain chances will return by late \nweek and into this weekend. Expect MinRHs of 15-25% across the \nwestern districts to 30- 50% over the next few afternoons. \nOvernight recovery will range from poor across SE California to \ngood across south-central AZ. Temperatures will range from near to\nslightly below normal over the next several days with lower \ndesert highs in the upper 90s to low 100s.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Salerno\nLONG TERM...Salerno \nAVIATION...RW \nFIRE WEATHER...Salerno\n",
